    <div class="section" id="s1">
      <p class="sec-num">01 —</p>
      <h2>What is Computer Networking?</h2>

      <p>A <strong>computer network</strong> is a collection of interconnected devices — computers, phones, printers,
        servers, routers — that can communicate and share resources with each other. Networks can be as small as two
        computers linked by a cable, or as vast as the global internet connecting billions of devices across every
        continent.</p>

      <p>Without networks, every computer would be an isolated island. Networking is what makes email, websites, video
        calls, cloud storage, online gaming, and every internet-connected service possible.</p>

      <div class="analogy">
        <p class="analogy-label">Analogy — The Postal System</p>
        <p>Think of computer networking like a postal system. Your data is the <strong>letter</strong>, IP addresses are
          <strong>street addresses</strong>, routers are <strong>sorting offices</strong>, protocols are <strong>the
            rules of packaging and addressing</strong>, and the internet is the <strong>entire postal network</strong>.
          Every concept in networking maps to something in how physical mail works.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Why Networking Matters</h3>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>Communication:</strong> Email, messaging, video conferencing — all rely on networks.</li>
        <li><strong>Resource sharing:</strong> Printers, storage, applications shared across organisations.</li>
        <li><strong>The World Wide Web:</strong> Every webpage you visit travels across networks.</li>
        <li><strong>Cloud computing:</strong> AWS, Azure, GCP are just someone else's networked computers.</li>
        <li><strong>IoT:</strong> Smart home devices, industrial sensors, medical equipment — all networked.</li>
      </ul>

