    <div class="section" id="s2">
      <p class="sec-num">02 —</p>
      <h2>A Brief History of Networks</h2>

      <p>Networking history is the story of humans relentlessly trying to communicate faster, farther, and more reliably
        — and each breakthrough enabling the next.</p>

      <div class="timeline">
        <div class="tl-item">
          <p class="tl-year">1969</p>
          <p class="tl-event"><strong>ARPANET</strong> — the forerunner of the internet — sends its first message
            between UCLA and Stanford. It crashes after two letters ("LO" of "LOGIN"), but the concept is proven. Four
            nodes by year end.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tl-item">
          <p class="tl-year">1973</p>
          <p class="tl-event"><strong>Ethernet</strong> invented at Xerox PARC by Robert Metcalfe. The foundational LAN
            technology still used in offices today. Also: ARPANET goes international (London, Norway).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tl-item">
          <p class="tl-year">1974</p>
          <p class="tl-event">Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn publish the <strong>TCP specification</strong> — "A Protocol for
            Packet Network Intercommunication." This is the foundation of the modern internet. Cerf and Kahn are known
            as the "Fathers of the Internet."</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tl-item">
          <p class="tl-year">1983</p>
          <p class="tl-event"><strong>TCP/IP becomes the standard</strong> protocol for ARPANET. January 1st 1983 is
            considered the official "birthday of the internet." The <strong>DNS</strong> (Domain Name System) is also
            introduced this year.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tl-item">
          <p class="tl-year">1984</p>
          <p class="tl-event">ISO publishes the <strong>OSI model</strong> — the 7-layer reference framework that gives
            networking a universal vocabulary for the next four decades.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tl-item">
          <p class="tl-year">1991</p>
          <p class="tl-event">Tim Berners-Lee invents the <strong>World Wide Web</strong> at CERN, built on top of
            TCP/IP. HTTP, HTML, and URLs are introduced. The internet gets a visual, navigable face.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tl-item">
          <p class="tl-year">1999–2003</p>
          <p class="tl-event"><strong>Wi-Fi (802.11b/g)</strong> goes mainstream. Broadband replaces dial-up.
            <strong>IPv6</strong> standardised (1998) in response to IPv4 address exhaustion concerns.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tl-item">
          <p class="tl-year">2008–now</p>
          <p class="tl-event">Mobile internet (3G→4G→5G), cloud computing, and IoT transform networking from
            infrastructure into the fabric of daily life. Software-Defined Networking (SDN) decouples control planes
            from hardware.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

