    <div class="section" id="s3">
      <p class="sec-num">03 —</p>
      <h2>The OSI Model — 7 Layers Explained</h2>

      <p>The <strong>Open Systems Interconnection (OSI) model</strong> is a conceptual framework published by ISO in
        1984 that divides network communication into <strong>seven distinct layers</strong>. Each layer has a specific
        job and communicates only with the layers directly above and below it.</p>

      <p>Think of it as an assembly line: each worker (layer) does one specific job, then passes the work to the next.
        The beauty of this design is <em>abstraction</em> — a web developer doesn't need to know how Ethernet works, and
        an Ethernet engineer doesn't need to know about HTTP.</p>

      <div class="analogy">
        <p class="analogy-label">Analogy — Sending a Physical Package</p>
        <p>Sending data is like shipping a package internationally. You write the letter (Application), seal it in an
          envelope (Presentation), put it in a box with packing (Session), label it with source/destination (Transport),
          add a routing slip for customs (Network), hand it to a courier (Data Link), and the courier physically drives
          it to the depot (Physical). Each "layer" handles its part without knowing the others' details.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="osi-stack">
        <div class="osi-row osi-hdr">
          <div class="osi-num">#</div>
          <div class="osi-name">Layer</div>
          <div class="osi-desc">Role &amp; Responsibility</div>
          <div class="osi-proto">Key Protocols</div>
        </div>
        <div class="osi-row l7">
          <div class="osi-num">7</div>
          <div class="osi-name">Application</div>
          <div class="osi-desc">The layer humans interact with. Provides network services directly to end-user
            applications — email, web browsing, file transfer.</div>
          <div class="osi-proto">HTTP, HTTPS, FTP, SMTP, DNS, SSH, DHCP, SNMP</div>
        </div>
        <div class="osi-row l6">
          <div class="osi-num">6</div>
          <div class="osi-name">Presentation</div>
          <div class="osi-desc">Translates data between application and network formats. Handles encryption,
            compression, and character encoding (ASCII → Unicode).</div>
          <div class="osi-proto">SSL/TLS, JPEG, MPEG, ASCII, UTF-8</div>
        </div>
        <div class="osi-row l5">
          <div class="osi-num">5</div>
          <div class="osi-name">Session</div>
          <div class="osi-desc">Manages connections (sessions) between applications — opening, maintaining, and closing
            dialogues. Handles authentication and reconnection.</div>
          <div class="osi-proto">NetBIOS, RPC, PPTP, SIP</div>
        </div>
        <div class="osi-row l4">
          <div class="osi-num">4</div>
          <div class="osi-name">Transport</div>
          <div class="osi-desc">End-to-end delivery between processes. Breaks data into segments, adds port numbers,
            handles error recovery (TCP) or speed (UDP).</div>
          <div class="osi-proto">TCP, UDP, SCTP</div>
        </div>
        <div class="osi-row l3">
          <div class="osi-num">3</div>
          <div class="osi-name">Network</div>
          <div class="osi-desc">Logical addressing and routing. Determines the best path for data to travel across
            multiple networks using IP addresses.</div>
          <div class="osi-proto">IP (v4/v6), ICMP, OSPF, BGP, RIP</div>
        </div>
        <div class="osi-row l2">
          <div class="osi-num">2</div>
          <div class="osi-name">Data Link</div>
          <div class="osi-desc">Node-to-node delivery on the same network. Frames data, uses MAC addresses, handles
            error detection and flow control.</div>
          <div class="osi-proto">Ethernet, Wi-Fi (802.11), PPP, ARP</div>
        </div>
        <div class="osi-row l1">
          <div class="osi-num">1</div>
          <div class="osi-name">Physical</div>
          <div class="osi-desc">The actual hardware — bits transmitted as electrical signals, light pulses, or radio
            waves across cables, fibre, or air.</div>
          <div class="osi-proto">Ethernet cable, USB, Fibre optic, Bluetooth radio, 5G</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout callout-blue">
        <p class="callout-title">Memory Aid</p>
        <p>Remember the layers top-to-bottom with: <strong>"All People Seem To Need Data Processing"</strong>
          (Application, Presentation, Session, Transport, Network, Data Link, Physical). Bottom-to-top: <strong>"Please
            Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away."</strong></p>
      </div>

      <h3>Encapsulation — How Data Moves Down the Stack</h3>
      <p>When you send data, each layer <strong>wraps</strong> the data with its own header (and sometimes trailer) — a
        process called <strong>encapsulation</strong>. The receiver <strong>decapsulates</strong> layer by layer in
        reverse.</p>

