      <h3>The Four TCP/IP Layers in Detail</h3>

      <h4>Layer 1 — Network Access (Link) Layer</h4>
      <p>Handles the physical transmission of data on a local network. Covers Ethernet, Wi-Fi (802.11), and all the
        hardware-level concerns. Corresponds to OSI Layers 1 and 2 combined.</p>

      <h4>Layer 2 — Internet Layer</h4>
      <p>The heart of TCP/IP. The <strong>Internet Protocol (IP)</strong> provides addressing and routing — determining
        the path data takes across multiple interconnected networks. Corresponds to OSI Layer 3. Key protocols:
        <code>IP</code>, <code>ICMP</code>, <code>ARP</code>, <code>BGP</code>.
      </p>

      <h4>Layer 3 — Transport Layer</h4>
      <p>Provides end-to-end communication between processes on different hosts. The two workhorses are
        <strong>TCP</strong> (reliable, ordered) and <strong>UDP</strong> (fast, best-effort). Ports identify which
        application on a host is communicating.
      </p>

      <h4>Layer 4 — Application Layer</h4>
      <p>Combines OSI's Application, Presentation, and Session layers. All user-facing protocols live here — HTTP,
        HTTPS, FTP, SSH, SMTP, DNS, DHCP. Application developers work almost exclusively at this layer.</p>

      <div class="callout callout-green">
        <p class="callout-title">OSI vs TCP/IP: Which Should You Learn?</p>
        <p><strong>Learn both.</strong> OSI is essential for conceptual understanding, troubleshooting methodology, and
          exam contexts (CompTIA, Cisco CCNA). TCP/IP is the model the real internet uses — your actual code and tools
          work within it. Professionals use OSI language daily ("this is a Layer 3 problem") while implementing TCP/IP.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

