       SECTION 5 — TCP vs UDP
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <div class="section" id="s5">
      <p class="sec-num">05 —</p>
      <h2>TCP vs UDP — When to Use Which</h2>

      <p>At the Transport layer, two protocols dominate: <strong>TCP</strong> (Transmission Control Protocol) and
        <strong>UDP</strong> (User Datagram Protocol). They represent two philosophies: <em>reliability vs speed</em>.
      </p>

      <div class="compare">
        <div class="compare-col tcp">
          <div class="compare-head">TCP — Transmission Control Protocol</div>
          <div class="compare-body">
            <ul>
              <li>Connection-oriented (3-way handshake)</li>
              <li>Guaranteed delivery — retransmits lost packets</li>
              <li>In-order delivery of data</li>
              <li>Flow control and congestion control</li>
              <li>Higher overhead, slightly slower</li>
              <li><strong>Use for:</strong> Web (HTTP/S), email, file transfer, SSH</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="compare-col udp">
          <div class="compare-head">UDP — User Datagram Protocol</div>
          <div class="compare-body">
            <ul>
              <li>Connectionless — no handshake needed</li>
              <li>No guaranteed delivery (fire and forget)</li>
              <li>No ordering guarantees</li>
              <li>No flow control</li>
              <li>Very low overhead, very fast</li>
              <li><strong>Use for:</strong> DNS, video streaming, VoIP, gaming, DHCP</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="analogy">
        <p class="analogy-label">Analogy — Phone Call vs Flyer Drop</p>
        <p><strong>TCP is like a phone call</strong> — you establish a connection, confirm both parties can hear, speak
          in turn, and confirm understanding. If a sentence is missed, you ask them to repeat it.<br><br><strong>UDP is
            like dropping flyers from a plane</strong> — you broadcast information without knowing who received it,
          without confirmation, and without resending missed ones. Fast and cheap, but unreliable.</p>
      </div>

