    <div class="section" id="s6">
      <p class="sec-num">06 —</p>
      <h2>Essential Network Protocols</h2>

      <p>A <strong>protocol</strong> is a set of rules governing how data is formatted, transmitted, received, and
        acknowledged. Protocols are the agreed-upon language that allows different devices and software to communicate
        reliably. Without protocols, the internet would be a tower of Babel.</p>

      <h3>Application Layer Protocols</h3>
      <div class="proto-grid">
        <div class="proto-card">
          <div class="proto-name">HTTP / HTTPS</div>
          <p>HyperText Transfer Protocol — the foundation of the Web. Every webpage you visit uses HTTP or its encrypted
            variant HTTPS (HTTP over TLS). A <code>GET</code> request fetches a resource; <code>POST</code> sends data
            to a server.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="proto-card">
          <div class="proto-name">DNS — Domain Name System</div>
          <p>Translates human-readable domain names (<code>google.com</code>) into IP addresses
            (<code>142.250.80.46</code>). Often called "the phonebook of the internet." Every internet connection starts
            with a DNS lookup.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="proto-card">
          <div class="proto-name">SMTP / IMAP / POP3</div>
          <p><strong>SMTP</strong> sends email between servers. <strong>IMAP</strong> syncs email to your client (leaves
            messages on server). <strong>POP3</strong> downloads and removes from server. Together they're the complete
            email stack.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="proto-card">
          <div class="proto-name">SSH — Secure Shell</div>
          <p>Encrypted protocol for remote command-line access to servers. Replaced Telnet (which sent everything in
            plaintext). Uses asymmetric cryptography for authentication. The workhorse of server administration.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="proto-card">
          <div class="proto-name">FTP / SFTP</div>
          <p>File Transfer Protocol transfers files between client and server. Plain FTP sends credentials unencrypted.
            <strong>SFTP</strong> (SSH File Transfer Protocol) is the secure replacement — same idea, encrypted tunnel.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="proto-card">
          <div class="proto-name">DHCP</div>
          <p>Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol automatically assigns IP addresses to devices on a network. Without
            DHCP, every device would need a manually configured IP. It's why your phone gets an IP when it joins Wi-Fi.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="proto-card">
          <div class="proto-name">SNMP</div>
          <p>Simple Network Management Protocol — allows network administrators to monitor and manage routers, switches,
            and servers. The backbone of network monitoring tools like Nagios, PRTG, and Datadog.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="proto-card">
          <div class="proto-name">NTP — Network Time Protocol</div>
          <p>Synchronises clocks across all networked devices to within milliseconds of each other. Critical for
            security logs, TLS certificates, Kerberos authentication, and distributed databases.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Network Layer Protocols</h3>

      <h4>IP — Internet Protocol</h4>
      <p>IP is the fundamental addressing and routing protocol of the internet. Every device on the internet has an IP
        address. IP is <strong>connectionless and unreliable</strong> — it makes no guarantees about delivery; that's
        TCP's job.</p>

