      <div class="callout callout-amber">
        <p class="callout-title">BGP — The Internet's Most Critical Protocol</p>
        <p><strong>BGP (Border Gateway Protocol)</strong> is the routing protocol that holds the entire internet
          together. It allows autonomous systems (ISPs, CDNs, companies) to advertise which IP address ranges they can
          reach. A BGP misconfiguration can accidentally make large parts of the internet unreachable — as happened in
          the 2021 Facebook outage, when Facebook accidentally withdrew its own BGP routes.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>TLS/SSL — The Security Layer</h3>
      <p><strong>Transport Layer Security (TLS)</strong> — formerly SSL — is the protocol that secures HTTPS, email, and
        most encrypted internet traffic. It sits between the Application and Transport layers, providing:</p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>Encryption:</strong> All data is encrypted so eavesdroppers see only gibberish.</li>
        <li><strong>Authentication:</strong> Digital certificates verify you're talking to the real server.</li>
        <li><strong>Integrity:</strong> Message authentication codes detect any tampering in transit.</li>
      </ul>

