      <p class="sec-num">07 —</p>
      <h2>IP Addressing, Subnetting &amp; DNS</h2>

      <h3>Understanding IP Addresses</h3>
      <p>An IPv4 address is <strong>32 bits</strong> written as four decimal numbers (0–255) separated by dots. Each
        number represents 8 bits (an <em>octet</em>). Two parts: the <strong>network portion</strong> (identifies the
        network) and the <strong>host portion</strong> (identifies the device).</p>

      <h3>Subnetting — Dividing Networks</h3>
      <p>Subnetting divides a large IP address space into smaller, manageable networks. A <strong>subnet mask</strong>
        (e.g. <code>255.255.255.0</code>) — or its CIDR notation equivalent (<code>/24</code>) — tells you which bits
        belong to the network.</p>

