      <h3>NAT — Network Address Translation</h3>
      <p>Your home has one public IP address (given by your ISP), but potentially dozens of devices.
        <strong>NAT</strong> allows all those devices to share a single public IP. The router maintains a translation
        table mapping private:port combinations to the single public IP.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout-teal">
        <p class="callout-title">Why NAT Saved the Internet</p>
        <p>IPv4 only has ~4.3 billion addresses — not enough for every device on Earth. NAT allowed billions of devices
          to share a relatively small pool of public IPs, delaying IPv4 exhaustion by decades. Every device on your home
          Wi-Fi uses private addresses (192.168.x.x) behind one NAT public IP.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>DNS — How Domain Names Resolve</h3>
      <p>DNS is a <strong>hierarchical, distributed database</strong> that maps domain names to IP addresses. It's
        organised into a tree, with the root at the top, then TLDs (.com, .org, .in), then second-level domains (google,
        amazon), then subdomains (mail, docs, www).</p>

