    <div class="section" id="s8">
      <p class="sec-num">08 —</p>
      <h2>Data Flow: What Happens When You Load a Webpage</h2>

      <p>Let's trace every single step from typing <code>https://www.example.com</code> in your browser to seeing the
        webpage. This single action involves every protocol and layer we've discussed.</p>

      <ol class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>URL parsing:</strong> Browser parses the URL — protocol (<code>https</code>), host
          (<code>www.example.com</code>), path (<code>/</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>DNS lookup:</strong> Browser checks cache, then asks the OS resolver, which queries the DNS
          hierarchy as described in Section 7. Result: <code>93.184.216.34</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>TCP connection:</strong> Browser initiates a TCP 3-way handshake with <code>93.184.216.34:443</code>
          (HTTPS port).</li>
        <li><strong>TLS handshake:</strong> After TCP is established, a TLS handshake negotiates encryption. Both sides
          agree on cipher suites, exchange certificates, and derive session keys.</li>
        <li><strong>HTTP request:</strong> Browser sends an encrypted HTTP/2 GET request through the TLS-secured TCP
          connection.</li>
        <li><strong>Routing:</strong> Packets traverse multiple routers. Each router reads the IP header, consults its
          routing table (BGP/OSPF), and forwards to the next hop.</li>
        <li><strong>Server processing:</strong> The web server receives the request, generates the HTML response.</li>
        <li><strong>HTTP response:</strong> Server sends back encrypted HTTP response (200 OK + HTML content).</li>
        <li><strong>TCP ACKs:</strong> Every segment is acknowledged. Lost segments are retransmitted.</li>
        <li><strong>Rendering:</strong> Browser receives, decrypts, and renders the HTML — possibly triggering dozens
          more DNS lookups and HTTP requests for CSS, JavaScript, images.</li>
      </ol>

