    <div class="section" id="s9">
      <p class="sec-num">09 —</p>
      <h2>Real-World Case Studies</h2>

      <div class="case-card">
        <p class="case-label">Case Study 01 — BGP Misconfiguration</p>
        <p class="case-title">Facebook Goes Dark (October 4, 2021)</p>
        <p><strong>What happened:</strong> During routine maintenance, a BGP configuration update at Facebook contained
          an error. Commands sent to the routers accidentally withdrew all of Facebook's BGP route advertisements —
          effectively telling the global internet that Facebook's IP address ranges no longer existed.</p>
        <p><strong>Impact:</strong> Facebook, Instagram, WhatsApp, and Messenger went completely offline for
          approximately 6 hours. 3.5 billion users affected. DNS servers for facebook.com became unreachable. Facebook's
          internal tools also failed — engineers couldn't remotely fix the issue and had to drive to datacentres with
          physical access. Estimated revenue loss: ~$60–100 million.</p>
        <p><strong>Technical detail:</strong> The BGP withdrawal was caused by a faulty configuration audit tool that
          had been given too broad permissions. The tool sent commands to take offline the backbone routers to verify
          connectivity — but the command actually executed. Because the DNS servers were on the same now-unreachable IP
          ranges, even the "is Facebook down?" queries couldn't reach Facebook's nameservers.</p>
        <p><strong>Lesson:</strong> BGP is extraordinarily powerful — and fragile. Changes must go through strict change
          management, staged rollouts, and automated rollback mechanisms. Never route your management infrastructure
          through the same systems being changed.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="case-card">
        <p class="case-label">Case Study 02 — DNS Attack</p>
        <p class="case-title">Dyn DNS DDoS — Half the Internet Goes Down (October 2016)</p>
        <p><strong>What happened:</strong> The Mirai botnet — made up of 600,000+ hacked IoT devices (cameras, DVRs,
          routers) — launched a massive DDoS attack against Dyn, a major DNS provider used by Twitter, Netflix, Reddit,
          GitHub, Airbnb, and hundreds of other major websites.</p>
        <p><strong>Impact:</strong> At its peak, over 1.2 Terabits/second of traffic targeted Dyn's infrastructure. DNS
          resolution for huge swathes of the internet failed. Users across the US and Europe couldn't access Twitter,
          Netflix, Reddit, and dozens of other services for up to 11 hours. The attack demonstrated that DNS is a
          critical — and underprotected — bottleneck.</p>
        <p><strong>Technical detail:</strong> The Mirai botnet flooded Dyn's DNS resolvers with lookup requests,
          overwhelming the servers' capacity to respond to legitimate queries. Because DNS is foundational to all
          internet navigation, taking out a major DNS provider is remarkably effective.</p>
        <p><strong>Lesson:</strong> DNS is not just a minor utility — it is load-bearing infrastructure for the
          internet. Use multiple, geographically distributed DNS providers (Anycast DNS). IoT device security must
          improve dramatically. UDP amplification attacks exploit DNS's stateless nature.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="case-card">
        <p class="case-label">Case Study 03 — TCP/IP Vulnerability</p>
        <p class="case-title">Heartbleed — When TLS Fails Everyone (2014)</p>
        <p><strong>What happened:</strong> A bug in OpenSSL's implementation of the TLS Heartbeat extension
          (CVE-2014-0160) allowed attackers to read 64KB of arbitrary memory from a server with each request —
          repeatedly. This could leak private keys, passwords, session tokens, and any sensitive data in memory.</p>
        <p><strong>Impact:</strong> OpenSSL was used by approximately 66% of all HTTPS servers at the time. An estimated
          17% of secure web servers were vulnerable. Over 500,000 servers exposed. Major services affected: Yahoo Mail,
          OKCupid, Imgur, LastPass, bank websites. Private SSL keys could be stolen silently with no trace in logs.</p>
        <p><strong>Technical detail:</strong> The Heartbeat extension allows a TLS peer to send a "heartbeat" request of
          a certain length. Heartbleed's bug: the server never validated that the claimed payload length matched the
          actual payload. An attacker could claim "this heartbeat is 65,535 bytes long" but send only 1 byte — the
          server would echo back 65,534 bytes of raw memory to fill the requested length.</p>
        <p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Even foundational, open-source security infrastructure has bugs. Audit your
          dependencies. Patch immediately when critical CVEs drop. Certificate rotation and secret invalidation
          processes must be rehearsed before you need them in an emergency.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="case-card">
        <p class="case-label">Case Study 04 — Network Design</p>
        <p class="case-title">How Cloudflare's Anycast Makes DNS Fast Everywhere</p>
        <p><strong>What happened (by design):</strong> Cloudflare operates its 1.1.1.1 public DNS resolver — and its
          entire CDN — using Anycast routing. In Anycast, the same IP address is announced from hundreds of data centres
          simultaneously via BGP. Packets are automatically routed to the nearest location.</p>
        <p><strong>Technical detail:</strong> When you query <code>1.1.1.1</code>, BGP routing automatically sends you
          to the closest of Cloudflare's 300+ data centres globally. The same IP answers from every continent. This
          means average DNS query times under 10ms globally, and DDoS resilience — an attack on one node is absorbed
          globally across 300 nodes.</p>
        <p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Anycast is one of the most powerful tools in large-scale network design. It
          demonstrates how BGP — the same protocol that caused the Facebook outage — can be used brilliantly to create
          resilient, globally distributed services when properly managed.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <hr class="divider">

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 10 — FUTURE
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <div class="section" id="s10">
      <p class="sec-num">10 —</p>
      <h2>The Future of Networking</h2>

      <div class="future-grid">
        <div class="future-card">
          <div class="future-icon">📶</div>
          <h4>5G &amp; Beyond</h4>
          <p>5G delivers sub-1ms latency and 20 Gbps peak speeds. Enables autonomous vehicles, remote surgery, smart
            cities, and massive IoT. 6G research already underway — targeting terahertz frequencies by 2030.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="future-card">
          <div class="future-icon">🌐</div>
          <h4>IPv6 Adoption</h4>
          <p>With ~46% of Google traffic already IPv6, the transition accelerates. IPv6 offers 340 undecillion
            addresses, built-in IPsec, better routing efficiency, and eliminates NAT complexity.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="future-card">
          <div class="future-icon">⚙️</div>
          <h4>SD-WAN &amp; SDN</h4>
          <p>Software-Defined Networking separates the control plane from hardware, enabling networks to be programmed
            like software. SD-WAN replaces expensive MPLS links with intelligent cloud-managed connections.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="future-card">
          <div class="future-icon">🔒</div>
          <h4>QUIC Protocol (HTTP/3)</h4>
          <p>Google's QUIC protocol replaces TCP+TLS with a unified, UDP-based protocol that eliminates head-of-line
            blocking and enables 0-RTT connection resumption. HTTP/3 (built on QUIC) is now 30% of all web traffic.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="future-card">
          <div class="future-icon">🛰️</div>
          <h4>Satellite Internet</h4>
          <p>Starlink, OneWeb, and Amazon Kuiper are building low-earth-orbit satellite constellations bringing &lt;50ms
            broadband everywhere on Earth — connecting the 2.7 billion people still without internet.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="future-card">
          <div class="future-icon">🔬</div>
          <h4>Quantum Networking</h4>
          <p>Quantum key distribution (QKD) creates theoretically unbreakable encryption using quantum entanglement.
            China has demonstrated 4,600km quantum network links. "Quantum internet" remains 10–20 years away.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>HTTP/3 and QUIC — Replacing the TCP Foundation</h3>
      <p>One of the most significant networking shifts is the replacement of TCP at the transport layer for web traffic.
        <strong>QUIC</strong> (Quick UDP Internet Connections), developed by Google and now an IETF standard, solves
        fundamental TCP limitations:
      </p>

      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>Head-of-line blocking eliminated:</strong> TCP stalls if one packet is lost. QUIC uses independent
          streams — a lost packet only blocks its own stream.</li>
        <li><strong>Connection establishment:</strong> TCP + TLS takes 2–3 round trips. QUIC combines transport and
          crypto — 1 RTT for new connections, 0 RTT for resuming.</li>
        <li><strong>Connection migration:</strong> QUIC connections survive IP address changes (switching from Wi-Fi to
          5G) — TCP connections break.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout callout-blue">
        <p class="callout-title">Key Takeaway</p>
        <p>Networking is not a solved problem — it is an evolving discipline. The OSI and TCP/IP models describe how
          today's internet works, but QUIC, IPv6, SDN, and 5G are actively rewriting those blueprints. Understanding the
          fundamentals makes it possible to reason about every new protocol and technology that builds on top of them.
        </p>
      </div>

